//
// `(T-Absent)` — a tuple is absent when EVERY member is null, not when its FIRST one is.
//
// `coalesce_not_null` carried a different convention: *"a tuple is null when its FIRST FIELD is
// its type's null sentinel"*.  That is the same test as the rule's for an all-or-nothing read —
// `OpGetVectorNullable` nulls every member at once — and a DIFFERENT one for every tuple that is
// partly present.  So `(null, 2) ?? (9, 9)` was discharged WHOLLY: the `2` was gone, on both
// backends, with no diagnostic.  A tuple whose first member is the one likely to be absent — a
// parse result, a lookup key beside its payload — loses the payload silently.
//
// ⚠ The cells are read from a `vector<(τ?, τ?)>` by a VARIABLE index, and that is the owner's
// 2026-09-16 ruling rather than a stylistic choice.  `?` and `??` discharge an ABSENCE, and only
// the in-flight `(τ₁, …, τₙ)?` an out-of-range read produces has one; a tuple a program writes
// down EXISTS whatever its members hold, so discharging one is refused and is
// `a-tuple-that-exists-has-nothing-to-discharge.loft`'s.  The member walk this file is about is
// untouched by that: an element of a `vector<(τ?, τ?)>` is partly present exactly as a written
// literal was, and the fold still has to read every member to see it.  The file previously
// spelled its cells as written literals, which is the spelling the ruling retired.
//
// Axes this file MOVES:  which member is absent (first · last · middle · none · all), the
//                        arity (2 · 3), the member kinds (integer · text · float · boolean ·
//                        nested tuple), whether a present member holds its type's ZERO
//                        (`false` · `0` · `""` · `0.0` — a value, not an absence), and whether
//                        the read HITS a stored element or MISSES the vector entirely.
// Axes it HOLDS FIXED:   the spelling of the question is `??` — `== null` is the same question
//                        under `(T-Absent)` and is `1477b`'s; and the discharge is on an
//                        in-flight read, never on a tuple the program holds.
//
// The all-null and index-miss cells are the CONTROLS: they passed under the old first-member
// convention too, so a cure that answered "present" for everything is caught by them, and a cure
// that answered "absent" for everything is caught by the partly-present cells.
//
// @falsified-at: 4f29a4730 — interpret exit 1 -> 0, interpret 5 assertion failures -> 0, native exit 1 -> 0, native 1 assertion failures -> 0
//   Every failure there is a partly-present tuple whose FIRST member is null and whose present
//   member came back as the default, while the two CONTROLS (all-null, index-miss) and every
//   cell whose first member is PRESENT pass.  So the old convention was right exactly when
//   member 0 was present and wrong exactly when it was not, which is what says the WALK and not
//   one shape was the defect.
//   ⚠ RE-MEASURED 2026-09-16 after the cells moved from written literals to in-flight reads
//   (the owner's tuple-absence ruling), because a receipt describes the cells that are HERE, not
//   the ones that were.  It still falsifies, which is the answer to the question the rewrite
//   raised: the fold lives in `coalesce_not_null` and is shared by both spellings, so reading an
//   element of a `vector<(τ?, τ?)>` catches it exactly as a written literal did.
//   ⚠ The control's assertion COUNT differs by backend — 5 on the interpreter, 1 on native — and
//   both are recorded rather than averaged: the cells are independent test functions, so what
//   differs is how many the old build got wrong, not where it stopped.
//   ⚠ NO durable `@falsified-by:` patch, and that is the tool's own verdict rather than an
//   omission: 4f29a4730 is 50 335 source lines from this tree, so a diff against it would
//   reintroduce everything fixed in between instead of this defect alone.
//   CHANNEL: the assertions + exit channels, on BOTH backends.
//   HOLDS: leak, panic, free-refusal and expectations are equal on both trees. Falsify prints
//   only the channels that MOVE and requires HERE to be clean.
//
//   A SECOND reference, because one cell needs it: on the intermediate build that had the member
//   walk but not the PEEL, `test_a_zero_valued_member_is_still_a_value` failed on its `boolean?`
//   assertion alone — a tuple with a `boolean?` member holding `false` took the default while the
//   same tuple with a BARE `boolean` member kept its value.  So the walk and the peel are two
//   cures and that cell distinguishes them; its `0`, `""` and `0.0` neighbours passed on that
//   build, which is what would have made the family look covered without the boolean in it.

// Each cell is its own test function, so a `--tests` run names EVERY broken one.  A single
// `main` halts at the first failing `assert`, which cannot tell "the member walk is wrong" from
// "one shape is" — and it is the ratio of broken partly-present cells to green controls that
// says which.

fn test_first_null_keeps_a_present_member() {
  v: vector<(integer?, integer?)> = [(null, 2)];
  i = 0;
  wa = v[i] ?? (9, 9);
  assert((wa.1 ?? -1) == 2, "first-null keeps the present 2, got {wa.1 ?? -1}");
  assert((wa.0 ?? -1) == -1, "first-null keeps its own absent member, got {wa.0 ?? -1}");
}

fn test_last_null_keeps_a_present_member() {
  v: vector<(integer?, integer?)> = [(1, null)];
  i = 0;
  wb = v[i] ?? (9, 9);
  assert((wb.0 ?? -1) == 1, "last-null keeps the present 1, got {wb.0 ?? -1}");
}

fn test_wholly_present_is_itself() {
  v: vector<(integer?, integer?)> = [(1, 2)];
  i = 0;
  wc = v[i] ?? (9, 9);
  assert((wc.0 ?? -1) == 1, "wholly present is itself, got {wc.0 ?? -1}");
}

// CONTROL — all null IS absent, which is what the old first-member convention also answered.
fn test_all_null_takes_the_default() {
  v: vector<(integer?, integer?)> = [(null, null)];
  i = 0;
  wd = v[i] ?? (9, 9);
  assert((wd.0 ?? -1) == 9, "all-null takes the default, got {wd.0 ?? -1}");
}

// Arity 3, absent at the FRONT and in the MIDDLE — a first-member test sees neither.
fn test_three_members_only_the_last_present() {
  v: vector<(integer?, integer?, integer?)> = [(null, null, 3)];
  i = 0;
  we = v[i] ?? (9, 9, 9);
  assert((we.2 ?? -1) == 3, "3-member with only the last present, got {we.2 ?? -1}");
}

fn test_three_members_with_a_middle_hole() {
  v: vector<(integer?, integer?, integer?)> = [(1, null, 3)];
  i = 0;
  wf = v[i] ?? (9, 9, 9);
  assert((wf.2 ?? -1) == 3, "3-member with a middle hole, got {wf.2 ?? -1}");
}

// A text member — a different sentinel, reached through the same recursion.
fn test_a_text_member_survives_a_null_first_member() {
  v: vector<(integer?, text?)> = [(null, "hi")];
  i = 0;
  wg = v[i] ?? (9, "zz");
  assert((wg.1 ?? "-") == "hi", "text member survives a null first member, got {wg.1 ?? \"-\"}");
}

// A NESTED tuple member: the walk has to bind it and ask the same question of it.
fn test_a_nested_tuple_member_is_walked() {
  v: vector<((integer?, integer?), integer?)> = [((null, 2), null)];
  i = 0;
  wh = v[i] ?? ((9, 9), 9);
  hi = wh.0;
  assert((hi.1 ?? -1) == 2, "nested present member survives, got {hi.1 ?? -1}");
}

fn test_a_nested_all_null_tuple_is_absent() {
  v: vector<((integer?, integer?), integer?)> = [((null, null), null)];
  i = 0;
  wi = v[i] ?? ((9, 9), 9);
  assert((wi.1 ?? -1) == 9, "nested all-null takes the default, got {wi.1 ?? -1}");
}

// A member holding its type's ZERO is a VALUE, not an absence — and this is the family that
// says the walk asks "does this member have a value", not "is this member truthy".  The
// `boolean?` cell is the one that was wrong: `coalesce_not_null`'s arms match some types in
// their BARE spelling only, because the top-level caller peels `Optional` before reaching them,
// so an UNPEELED member type missed its arm and fell to the generic truthiness convert.  `0`,
// `""` and `0.0` were right there anyway, which is exactly why the boolean needs its own cell —
// three green neighbours are what would have made this look covered.
fn test_a_zero_valued_member_is_still_a_value() {
  i = 0;
  vb: vector<(integer?, boolean?)> = [(null, false)];
  wb = vb[i] ?? (9, true);
  assert((wb.0 ?? -1) == -1, "a `false` member keeps the tuple, got default {wb.0 ?? -1}");
  vi: vector<(integer?, integer?)> = [(null, 0)];
  wi = vi[i] ?? (9, 9);
  assert((wi.0 ?? -1) == -1, "a `0` member keeps the tuple, got default {wi.0 ?? -1}");
  vt: vector<(integer?, text?)> = [(null, "")];
  wt = vt[i] ?? (9, "zz");
  assert((wt.0 ?? -1) == -1, "an empty-text member keeps the tuple, got default {wt.0 ?? -1}");
  vf: vector<(integer?, float?)> = [(null, 0.0)];
  wf = vf[i] ?? (9, 9.0);
  assert((wf.0 ?? -1) == -1, "a `0.0` member keeps the tuple, got default {wf.0 ?? -1}");
}

// CONTROL — an index MISS nulls every member at once, so the answer is absent, and an in-range
// read is present.  This pair is where the old convention agreed with the rule; it must not move.
fn test_an_index_miss_and_an_in_range_read() {
  v: vector<(integer, integer)> = [(1, 2)];
  miss = 5;
  hit = 0;
  wm = v[miss] ?? (9, 9);
  assert(wm.0 == 9, "an index miss takes the default, got {wm.0}");
  wt = v[hit] ?? (9, 9);
  assert(wt.0 == 1, "an in-range read is itself, got {wt.0}");
}
